  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1227 PM CST Mon Nov 23 2026\n\nWe have been pleasantly sandwiched today between mid level clouds \njust to our south and low clouds just to our northeast. While the \nlow clouds north could drift into the Victoria Crossroads, most of \nus will see increased cloud cover through the afternoon from the \nclouds south as mid-level moisture advects back into the region. \nThis increasing moisture will set us up for some mainly light rain \nand drizzle during the day tomorrow as a mid-level (around 700 \nmb/305K) isentropic lift pattern sets up, though lower levels are a \nbit dry. Some enhanced pockets of rain/showers will be possible \nthough as isentropic isn't the only player given a 500-700mb \nshortwave passing by. Will see most of this rain come to an end by \nthe evening hours as the trough passes and mid-level winds shift \nnorthwestward. The dreary day will also be feature highs in the 50s \nand a moderate to strong northerly wind.\n\nAs drier air settles in Wednesday and Thursday and high pressure \nsettles in will expect a couple of days with well below normal \ntemperatures. High temperatures Wednesday and Thursday will struggle \nto reach 60 and low temperatures will fall into the 30s and lower \n40s. The sun will be out though, and winds light. Chances for \ntemperatures inland to fall to freezing are non-zero, but pretty low \n- around 15%. Would not be surprised if some low/drainage areas of \nLa Salle/McMullen counties briefly touched freezing Thursday \nmorning. \n\nBy the end of the week will return to a more southerly flow with \nhigh temperatures back into the 70s",
